When a Spring Boot application must call 9+ third-party APIs, each with different request/response formats, the biggest mistakes are:

      Calling APIs sequentially.
      Mixing API-specific logic into business services.
      Using blocking clients (RestTemplate).
      Not implementing retries, timeouts, circuit breakers.
      Returning data only after all APIs finish.
      Not using Java 21 virtual threads or reactive processing.
======================================================================================
For a Google/Amazon/Microsoft-level implementation, the architecture should be:


                   Client Request
                          |
                          v
                Aggregation Controller
                          |
                          v
                 Aggregation Service
                          |
     -------------------------------------------------
     |       |        |        |       |      |      |
     v       v        v        v       v      v      v
  API-1   API-2    API-3    API-4   API-5  API-6  API-7
     |       |        |        |       |      |      |
     -------------------------------------------------
                          |
                          v
                Response Aggregator
                          |
                          v
                   Final Response

================================================================================
The implementation combines several well-known design patterns rather than relying on a single pattern.

1. Strategy Pattern ⭐ (Most Important)
2. Factory + Registry Pattern
3. Adapter Pattern
4. Facade Pattern ⭐

5. Template Method Pattern (Optional Enhancement)

8. Proxy Pattern

9. Composite Aggregation Pattern

For a production-grade Spring Boot API Aggregator, the architecture typically looks like:

Facade
   |
   +---- Factory/Registry
              |
              +---- Strategy
                        |
                        +---- Template Method
                        |
                        +---- Adapter
                        |
                        +---- Proxy
                        |
                        +---- Decorator
   |
   +---- Composite Aggregator
   |
   +---- Builder
Interview Answer (2-minute version)

"For integrating multiple third-party APIs, I would use a combination of Facade, Strategy, Factory/Registry, and Adapter patterns. 
The Facade acts as the aggregation layer, Strategy encapsulates API-specific logic, 
Factory/Registry dynamically resolves the correct API client, and Adapter converts external request/response formats into internal DTOs. 
For resilience, I would apply the Decorator pattern using Resilience4j (Retry, Circuit Breaker, Timeout, Bulkhead). 
Finally,  I would use the Builder pattern to construct the aggregated response and a Composite-style aggregation approach using CompletableFuture or 
WebClient parallel execution to achieve optimal performance."

This is the pattern combination commonly seen in large-scale Spring Boot systems handling many external integrations.
